And if I found myself in that position again,

258
00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.470
that I would chill out and make it about family

259
00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.570
and I needed to break away from New York City

260
00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:46.530
in order to do that.

261
00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:50.280
So the revisionist history of it is like, absolutely,

262
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.332
mission accomplished and I have no doubt

263
00:10:53.332 --> 00:10:55.743
that I'll be able to bring some of my chill

264
00:10:55.743 --> 00:10:56.580
back to New York.

265
00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:57.647
That was my question.

266
00:10:57.647 --> 00:10:59.820
'Cause you've lived on these polarities,

267
00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:01.590
but can you blend those

268
00:11:01.590 --> 00:11:06.476
and still maintain a modicum of work-life balance?

269
00:11:06.476 --> 00:11:09.240
Or are you afraid when you return to New York,

270
00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.380
you're gonna hit the ground running

271
00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.263
and fall back into that maniacal pattern?

272
00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.880
Call me naive and when we do our fifth podcast together,

273
00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.010
you can literally call me naive, if I'm wrong,

274
00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:21.120
but I think I can.

275
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.120
And the reason why is, I only ever knew one version of,

276
00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.680
not just life in New York City, but life.

277
00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:31.410
Like I moved to New York City when I was 19, 20 years old,

278
00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:34.470
totally broke and it was a dead sprint.

279
00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:37.500
It was a dead sprint and I did find success.

280
00:11:37.500 --> 00:11:39.360
And I found sort of financial freedom.

281
00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:41.520
I have financial security, a family,

282
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.410
all those things I was looking for.

283
00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:47.160
But it was after 19 years of fighting.

284
00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.830
And so then, after 19 years of fighting,

285
00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:51.303
I'm still in the city, but I've achieved all these things

286
00:11:51.303 --> 00:11:54.780
that I had hoped to achieve, I couldn't just stop.

287
00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:56.250
I didn't know what to do.

288
00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.430
So by forcing myself out of the city coming to Los Angeles,

289
00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.050
I was able to sort of catch my breath,

290
00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:03.780
look around and be like, okay, I get it now.

291
00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:08.520
And I think that it's a very different version of me

292
00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:09.990
going back to New York

293
00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:12.330
and I'll be able to appreciate the city

294
00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:13.380
for different reasons

295
00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.120
and I'll be able to appreciate family life

296
00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:16.590
for different reasons.

297
00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:19.080
My life is certainly much more set up now

298
00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:21.707
to be about being a dad and taking care of my kids

299
00:12:21.707 --> 00:12:24.780
and being a husband than it was three years ago

300
00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:25.770
when I left New York.

301
00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.293
So I do think I'll be able to find that.

302
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:33.480
Your marriage survived the intensity of the vlog period

303
00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.670
and it also survived the intensity

304
00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:39.390
of just basically cohabitating 24/7, right?

305
00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.220
Like being cooped up.
Yeah.

306
00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.080
That's a pretty good stress test for-

307
00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.450
Survive is such a generous word

308
00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:48.210
as far as the binary of like, we are not divorced.

309
00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:49.770
That is fact.
Right.

310
00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.920
But there is such a, a marriage is such a rubber band

311
00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:55.290
and sometimes, it's really stretched to the edge

312
00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.600
and sometimes, you've got a lot of slack.

313
00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.780
And I think, yeah, during the vlog period,

314
00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:01.890
working seven days a week,

315
00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:06.630
it was at its absolute max you could probably stretch

316
00:13:06.630 --> 00:13:09.420
that rubber band and certainly there've been struggles

317
00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:10.680
in the last couple years.
Yeah.

318
00:13:10.680 --> 00:13:12.750
Like, keeping them home from school is a major-

319
00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:16.560
Is there a sense of what the projects are when you return

320
00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.270
that you're gonna start executing on?

321
00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.523
Majorly.
Yeah.

322
00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:25.950
To give this answer context, but it's like,

323
00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:29.580
I started my career somewhat in the traditional media space.

324
00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:32.190
I was making movies that went to film festivals.

325
00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.950
I was producing feature films that went to film festivals

326
00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:36.600
and played in theaters.

327
00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.850
I had a TV show in HBO.

328
00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:43.020
And then with all of that sort of traction in my career,

329
00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.910
I ultimately got frustrated

330
00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.260
'cause I wasn't able to do what I wanted to do.

331
00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:49.500
So I ran to YouTube in like 2010, 2012.

332
00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.730
And I built this, you know,

333
00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.740
that's when I really found success.

334
00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:56.790
And then I took that success and I moved here to LA

335
00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:58.440
and I was like, well, let me make another feature film.

336
00:13:58.440 --> 00:13:59.323
And I made it another,

337
00:13:59.323 --> 00:14:01.530
a feature length documentary the last,

338
00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:03.080
it took me three years to make.

339
00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.150
And if there's one takeaway from this movie

340
00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:08.610
that premiered a film festival very well received,

341
00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.380
it's that I fucking hate it.

342
00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.320
I hate it. I hate everything about-

343
00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:14.423
You hate the traditional mechanisms of-

344
00:14:14.423 --> 00:14:17.760
I hate it. I hate the act of creation.

345
00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:21.510
I hate the act of distribution. That's not an act.

346
00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:24.169
But I hate the means of distribution. I hate all of it.

347
00:14:24.169 --> 00:14:25.050
You hate the creation?

348
00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.930
I hate the creation of feature films. I don't like that.

349
00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:28.860
I don't like that process.

350
00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.330
I don't like the collaborative process.

351
00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:32.250
I don't like working with others.

352
00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.680
I worked with the most brilliant editors

353
00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:37.980
in the world on my doc, they are lovely,

354
00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:39.378
who's one of my favorite people.

355
00:14:39.378 --> 00:14:42.539
And this is an excuse, I hate all of it.

356
00:14:42.539 --> 00:14:44.156
And when I say I hate all of it,

357
00:14:44.156 --> 00:14:47.220
I'm looking at a binary and that is like,

358
00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.890
what the process of creating in that world is,

359
00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.320
which is I had a really great team and all this.

360
00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:55.080
Or just sitting alone in my studio by myself

361
00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:00.080
with a laptop and a camera, that I fucking love. I love it.

362
00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:02.760
So what's beneath that? What's behind that?

363
00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.070
Like why is it that you're so frustrated

364
00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:07.770
by the collaborative aspect of film making

365
00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:10.860
or kind of the shenanigans that come along

366
00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:14.130
for the ride with making a movie in the traditional way?

367
00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.780
It could be the lack of agency.

368
00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:20.780
It could be because I'm able to look at a project

369
00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:23.400
that's done collaboratively and be like,

370
00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:24.540
wow, if I see weaknesses,

371
00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:27.993
I'm able to say they're somebody else's fault.

372
00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:32.130
You know, dismiss aspects of my work

373
00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:33.930
that I don't think are a hundred percent

374
00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:36.780
as somebody else's responsibility.

375
00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:38.310
I don't know the answer.

376
00:15:38.310 --> 00:15:41.820
And I can tell you, like, I know a number of fine artists

377
00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.120
that both were completely alone.

378
00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.010
They sit in their studios by themselves,

379
00:15:47.010 --> 00:15:48.647
know music and they paint.

380
00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:52.050
And then I know a bunch of fine artists that have, like,

381
00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:54.300
I have one artist friend who's a sculptor in New York City.

382
00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:57.063
I think he has like 18 or 20 people working for him.

383
00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:00.420
And that is where he thrives.

384
00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:01.940
And when it comes to filmmaking,

385
00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:04.440
I'm that guy who wants to sit alone.

386
00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:07.757
I wanna like, my favorite is even like I have, you know,

387
00:16:07.757 --> 00:16:09.960
I have a decent sized studio in New York City

388
00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:11.460
that requires a second set of hands

389
00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:13.590
to help me operate the place and get the lights on

390
00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:15.450
and make things work and things like that.

391
00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:20.070
When I'm in my studio, that individual, my studio manager,

392
00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:21.840
can't physically be in the studio.

393
00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:24.450
Typically, I have a separate space for them to sit all day

394
00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:28.053
because that's how much I cherish being alone.

395
00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:33.060
I relate to that deeply, but I've had to kind of grow

396
00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:35.340
and evolve in order to be able

397
00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:37.380
to continue doing this thing that I love.

398
00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:40.080
Because, as you know, it was me in a traveling suitcase.

399
00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.910
I think, Tyler, my stepson,

400
00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:43.380
came with me one time to your studio.

401
00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:46.200
Another time, Mathis came, who just graduated high school,

402
00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.430
by the way, which is crazy.

403
00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:48.480
Unbelievable.

404
00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:52.230
But in order, and being a total control freak over

405
00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:56.160
like every detail of the production and the editing

406
00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.800
and everything, I would do all of it myself,

407
00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.950
but it just became untenable.

408
00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:03.960
Like I wanted to be able to continue to do this

409
00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:07.350
and I had to figure out how to get comfortable

410
00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:10.740
letting go of certain things and empowering other people.

411
00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:12.060
Like you're here in the studio

412
00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:14.940
and you see there's other people here that help me now.

413
00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:18.060
And that's allowed me, it's challenging at times

414
00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:21.060
'cause sometimes it's like, I would do it this way,

415
00:17:21.060 --> 00:17:23.160
but I want them to feel

416
00:17:23.160 --> 00:17:26.790
like they're contributing meaningfully in a creative way.

417
00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.830
And I have made peace with that

418
00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:32.940
and it allows me to still maintain this love affair

419
00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:34.650
with the most important part of it,

420
00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:36.270
which is having the conversation

421
00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:37.637
so I can continue to do it,

422
00:17:37.637 --> 00:17:40.530
'cause it's 10 years that I've been doing this.

423
00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.440
So I understand that tension and that conundrum.

424
00:17:43.440 --> 00:17:45.690
And I think when you're that,

425
00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:49.350
like when you're a personality type like yourself,

426
00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.960
that's where you get into the problems with burnout

427
00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:54.120
because you can't, it's difficult to scale

428
00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.540
what you're doing if everything, every decision,

429
00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:02.310
you're the bottleneck on every iota of the creative process.

430
00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:03.650
But that's what being an artist is.

431
00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.840
If I limit it though to, I hear you.

432
00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:11.040
But I think if you limit it to the creative process,

433
00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:13.290
meaning that absolute agency

434
00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:17.700
that working singularly, that lone wolf thing.

435
00:18:17.700 --> 00:18:20.460
If you limit it only to the creative process,

436
00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:21.960
I think it's a very different equation.

437
00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:23.730
So that even that artist who sits alone

438
00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:26.310
with no music, painting by herself

439
00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:27.450
'cause she doesn't like to work, well,

440
00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:29.190
she still has an art dealer.

441
00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.200
She still has somebody who makes sure

442
00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:32.910
that her cellphone bill is paid every month.

443
00:18:32.910 --> 00:18:36.480
She still has people who support and enable her career.

444
00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.300
I still work with brilliant people at WME

445
00:18:39.300 --> 00:18:42.270
that I've been with forever that help negotiate brand deals

446
00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.880
and the sorts of things that keep the lights on.

447
00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.320
I still wanna have a studio manager

448
00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:48.270
to help me keep that studio functional.

449
00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:50.820
But when it comes to the creative process,

450
00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:52.230
the thing that I love the most,

451
00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:54.690
I have no interest in sharing it.

452
00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:57.873
I want it to just be me.

453
00:18:59.010 --> 00:19:00.540
Yeah, it hearkens to that thing

454
00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:01.770
I've heard you say many times,

455
00:19:01.770 --> 00:19:04.500
like managers wanna have meetings

456
00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:07.890
and creators just want to be left alone to create, right?

457
00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:09.390
And on some level,

458
00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:12.420
to create something that scales beyond you,

459
00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:13.560
you kind of need both.

460
00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.660
But if you're tipping heavily

461
00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.270
towards the creators just wanna create,

462
00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:21.180
there's no room or tolerance for meetings

463
00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:24.480
and phone calls and emails and all that kind of stuff

464
00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:27.240
that comes along for the ride

465
00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:29.490
when you achieve some level of success.

466
00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:33.420
Yeah, I also think that there's, uniquely,

467
00:19:33.420 --> 00:19:35.310
I'm staring at an opportunity that I don't know

468
00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:36.870
that I've propitiated in the past,

469
00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:41.760
but so much of my reach or whatever the quantifiers are

470
00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:46.320
for success on YouTube, so much of that was done

471
00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:51.240
at an insane pace when I was doing 365 videos a year

472
00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.070
for years on end.

473
00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.850
800 videos strong day after day after day

474
00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.220
that I only really understood that cadence.

475
00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:01.080
And I haven't really made any videos

476
00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:03.300
in the last couple years, I've made a few here and there,

477
00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:07.440
but in going back, when I go back rather,

478
00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.300
I'm definitely not interested or capable

479
00:20:09.300 --> 00:20:12.480
or able ever to go back to that cadence

480
00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:13.410
of seven days a week.

481
00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:17.550
I'm not interested in having the videos

482
00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:21.450
be so sort of self-centered and really just about me,

483
00:20:21.450 --> 00:20:24.843
my creative interests, my interests in the sort of themes

484
00:20:24.843 --> 00:20:28.020
that I hope to share have expanded a lot.

485
00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:30.633
So I think that I can be as,

486
00:20:31.590 --> 00:20:34.020
I think that I can find a level of success

487
00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:37.200
by just making it about quality

488
00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:40.080
and just trying to tell the stories

489
00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.680
that I wanna tell as best as I can possibly tell them.

490
00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.360
And if some days that's a little bullshit story

491
00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:47.190
about nothing, great.
Right.

492
00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.020
In some days, it's about bigger

493
00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:50.520
and more existential topics

494
00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:51.990
that I lay in bed thinking about every day.

495
00:20:51.990 --> 00:20:53.880
That's also fantastic.

496
00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:57.630
And is that still in the context of identifying yourself

497
00:20:57.630 --> 00:20:59.790
as a YouTuber like this identity?

498
00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.230
Or have you kind of grown beyond that?

499
00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:04.530
Well, what's funny is I think that that identity

500
00:21:04.530 --> 00:21:07.230
has evolved so much in the last couple years.

501
00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:09.990
Like YouTuber's not really is much of a thing anymore.

502
00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:14.370
Like I think YouTube has really abandoned defining that.

503
00:21:14.370 --> 00:21:16.800
YouTube as a company has given up on trying to define that,

504
00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:18.930
I think necessarily so.

505
00:21:18.930 --> 00:21:22.200
I think that this awful title of influencer

506
00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.000
has sort of swept up everything.

507
00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:27.210
And I think that now to be successful in social media,

508
00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:31.230
it's predicated on sort of dominating all the platforms.

509
00:21:31.230 --> 00:21:33.450
I don't see myself as falling into that category.

510
00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:36.930
In fact, I think, and this sounds so self righteous

511
00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.690
and obnoxious, but I'll say it gently like,

512
00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:43.050
I still see myself as a filmmaker

513
00:21:43.050 --> 00:21:44.880
who just really likes to use YouTube

514
00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:46.893
as a means of distribution.

515
00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:51.030
You know, my wife and I were invited

516
00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.673
to the Cannes Film Festival three weeks ago.

