to show you where he got lunch today.

1205
00:50:28.860 --> 00:50:31.140
He's just fucking living his life, man,

1206
00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:33.447
with no desire to share that.

1207
00:50:33.447 --> 00:50:35.640
And his movies are great.

1208
00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:36.990
You know, Spike Jonze,

1209
00:50:36.990 --> 00:50:40.890
who is one of my favorite creative brains in the universe,

1210
00:50:40.890 --> 00:50:43.230
he's not out there trying to get clicks or likes

1211
00:50:43.230 --> 00:50:47.220
and share cute, funny, little things on Twitter so we can,

1212
00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:48.660
for some external validation

1213
00:50:48.660 --> 00:50:50.520
or remind the world that he exists,

1214
00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:52.230
he's just living his life.

1215
00:50:52.230 --> 00:50:55.620
And then making these brilliant creative things,

1216
00:50:55.620 --> 00:50:58.800
whether they're short movies or Jackass 4

1217
00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:01.590
or like amazing Apple commercials or feature films

1218
00:51:01.590 --> 00:51:04.050
or whatever it is that Spike Jonze is up to.

1219
00:51:04.050 --> 00:51:06.090
And I think that there's a lot of brilliant actors

1220
00:51:06.090 --> 00:51:07.443
that are similar.

1221
00:51:08.520 --> 00:51:11.340
You know, I've sat behind Tom Hanks,

1222
00:51:11.340 --> 00:51:14.340
who's like the greatest ever at the Cannes Film Festival.

1223
00:51:14.340 --> 00:51:15.940
So cool seeing him in real life.

1224
00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:19.650
That motherfucker doesn't have Twitter, does he?

1225
00:51:19.650 --> 00:51:20.483
There's no way.

1226
00:51:20.483 --> 00:51:21.750
No, he does.
Does he tweet?

1227
00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:24.900
Yeah, and he signs his tweets, Hanx with an X.

1228
00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:25.733
Okay. Yeah.

1229
00:51:25.733 --> 00:51:26.566
I don't know how often he tweets-

1230
00:51:26.566 --> 00:51:28.500
But he mostly posts pictures of his typewriter.

1231
00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:30.090
Right.

1232
00:51:30.090 --> 00:51:31.353
But he's not out there.

1233
00:51:31.353 --> 00:51:33.600
He's not fighting to share his life.

1234
00:51:33.600 --> 00:51:35.730
Brad Pitt doesn't work every day.

1235
00:51:35.730 --> 00:51:37.410
So people will give him

1236
00:51:37.410 --> 00:51:40.710
as many hearts as possible on Instagram.

1237
00:51:40.710 --> 00:51:43.170
And I think that, at least what I take from that,

1238
00:51:43.170 --> 00:51:45.870
and call me naive and you're probably right, but,

1239
00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:48.750
I think that it's a demonstration

1240
00:51:48.750 --> 00:51:51.870
of where these brilliant creatives, where their focus is,

1241
00:51:51.870 --> 00:51:56.700
and their focus is on what they do great and their craft.

1242
00:51:56.700 --> 00:52:01.410
And I think that in social media,

1243
00:52:01.410 --> 00:52:03.120
there's sort of this weird perversion

1244
00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:04.650
where it's less about your craft

1245
00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:07.890
and it's more about marketing yourself, branding yourself.

1246
00:52:07.890 --> 00:52:09.930
So it's a stupid fuckin' buzzword, but like,

1247
00:52:09.930 --> 00:52:11.080
that's what it's about.

1248
00:52:11.940 --> 00:52:15.540
And I had to do that. I don't think I could have succeeded.

1249
00:52:15.540 --> 00:52:16.590
And maybe I'm making excuses,

1250
00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:18.270
but I don't think I could have had the success I had

1251
00:52:18.270 --> 00:52:19.413
if I hadn't done that.

1252
00:52:20.940 --> 00:52:25.940
But I'm like, I'm older now and I'm much more confident now

1253
00:52:26.310 --> 00:52:28.813
and is there a world where I can both be successful

1254
00:52:28.813 --> 00:52:31.050
in this creative space, which is social media,

1255
00:52:31.050 --> 00:52:32.670
without being that,

1256
00:52:32.670 --> 00:52:36.210
because that's never been exciting to me.

1257
00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:39.540
And it's scarier to me now than it's ever been.

1258
00:52:39.540 --> 00:52:43.260
Well, certainly, you can't create anything timeless

1259
00:52:43.260 --> 00:52:45.840
unless you have the facility or the skill

1260
00:52:45.840 --> 00:52:47.700
to meet out distractions.

1261
00:52:47.700 --> 00:52:50.880
And you have to be much more deliberate and intentional

1262
00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:54.210
about making sure that you have the time

1263
00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:59.070
to invest in your creativity without having to like

1264
00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:01.350
just be scrolling all the time.

1265
00:53:01.350 --> 00:53:03.240
It used to be like we were bored.

1266
00:53:03.240 --> 00:53:05.310
Now boredom is optional

1267
00:53:05.310 --> 00:53:08.610
and most people opt for mindless scrolling.

1268
00:53:08.610 --> 00:53:11.160
And it's so addictive that you have to like,

1269
00:53:11.160 --> 00:53:13.350
you know, you had to go and get a different phone

1270
00:53:13.350 --> 00:53:15.210
in order to protect yourself, right?

1271
00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:18.030
Like it's crazy that that's the society that we live in.

1272
00:53:18.030 --> 00:53:22.170
Our weak, feeble monkey brains just cannot compete

1273
00:53:22.170 --> 00:53:24.240
with those algorithms, it is so,

1274
00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:28.050
and I try to read a book, I can't read a book,

1275
00:53:28.050 --> 00:53:30.940
read a book or look at TikTok, it's like,

1276
00:53:30.940 --> 00:53:32.490
there's no competition there.

1277
00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:34.710
It's like having a bowl of kale

1278
00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:36.960
or like having a dozen donuts.

1279
00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:40.770
Like if there's no, your brain wants one thing.

1280
00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:43.263
